<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>

    <PackageId>TheKrystalShip.KGSM.Lib</PackageId>
    <Authors>Heisenberg</Authors>
    <Version>1.36.0</Version>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>1.31.0 — Phase 1 settings spine: IWatchdogClient gains EnableAsync/DisableAsync/GetEnabledNamesAsync, wiring the per-instance autostart (boot-enable) control through the watchdog's /enable·/disable·/enabled endpoints. The lib exposes the full boot-set surface: enable a name, disable it, query the current set. — 1.29.0 — Player-presence generalization (session correlation + addr/reason). Extends InstancePlayerJoinedData with PlayerAddr (string?, a real ip:port on direct-socket games — null on Steam-relay/P2P games) and SessionKey (string?, the opaque per-session correlation token key ?? addr ?? id ?? name — always non-empty on the real wire, nullable here only to mirror the existing declaration style); InstancePlayerLeftData gains the same two plus Reason (string?, the disconnect reason when the game's log carries one, e.g. RemoteConnectionClose/App_Min — kick/ban classification of this vocabulary is deferred to a future version). This is the consumer-side vocabulary for the watchdog's generalized native log-scraping session map (join/leave correlation, dedup of doubled/repeated log lines) validated against four real games (Stationeers, romestead, Valheim, Core Keeper) — see player-presence-contract.md. Both types are already registered in KgsmJsonContext (properties on a registered type need no new [JsonSerializable]), so this is additive and non-breaking: a pre-1.29.0 payload without these fields deserializes them as null, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (no reflection). VOCABULARY ONLY — the lib emits nothing here; emission is the watchdog's (string-based EmitWithProvenance). — 1.28.0 — Self-healing event-socket listener (UnixSocketClient). The listener previously bound its unix socket ONCE at startup and looped on AcceptAsync forever; if the socket FILE was unlinked out from under the process (observed on /run tmpfs cleanup with no service restart), the kernel kept the LISTEN object alive so AcceptAsync never errored — but the filesystem path was gone, so producers' (kgsm/watchdog) outbound connect() calls silently ENOENT'd and events stopped flowing until a manual restart (the kgsm-api audit pipeline went dark this way). StartListeningAsync now runs a supervisory loop: a background watcher polls File.Exists on the bound path every 5s and, when it disappears, cancels accept and RE-BINDS in place (re-running the existing EnsureSocketPathClean + bind), so the listener recovers with no process restart. A bind/accept failure that isn't a shutdown (e.g. the whole runtime dir vanished) backs off 2s and retries rather than dying permanently. Same-path replacement by another process isn't separately detected (rare) — the next re-bind reclaims the path regardless. Behaviour-only for the happy path (bind→accept unchanged); no API/signature/wire change; reflection-free / Native-AOT-safe (File.Exists + Task.Delay + linked CancellationTokenSource, no new type). Benefits every event-socket consumer (kgsm-api/monitor/bot) on adoption. — 1.27.0 — IInstanceService.Install gains an optional int? port (trailing param, after provenance, so existing positional callers are unaffected). When non-null it is passed to KGSM as `install --port &lt;N&gt;`, overriding the blueprint's primary game port for the new instance; null keeps the blueprint's declared port(s). Pairs with the kgsm change adding `--port` to install/create (threaded into the instance config's `ports=` via __override_primary_port, which rewrites the primary port while preserving protocols, ranges, and secondary ports). The control panel (kgsm-api M8·b install) forwards the SPA's install-form Game Port through this. Additive and non-breaking: the new param defaults to null, implementers of IInstanceService update the Install signature in lockstep, AOT-safe (no new type, no reflection — int formatted to a plain arg). — 1.26.0 — IInstanceService.GetAllOrNull(): the roster read that distinguishes a FAILED engine read from a genuinely empty one. ExecuteForJson returns null on a non-zero exit OR unparseable output and the deserialized value on success; KGSM emits "{}" for an empty roster (deserializes to a NON-null empty dictionary), so null here unambiguously means the read failed, never "zero instances". GetAll() keeps its lenient "?? []" (unchanged for callers that don't care); GetAllOrNull() preserves the distinction for surfaces that must not treat a transient read failure as zero servers (kgsm-api: GET /servers → 503 keep-stale instead of 200 [], and the DomainPump → skip the tick instead of tombstoning every instance). Additive and non-breaking (new interface method; implementers add it in lockstep), AOT-safe (no new type, no reflection — Dictionary&lt;string,Instance&gt; already registered). Residual not addressed: a per-instance _print_info_json failure makes kgsm `continue` and emit a PARTIAL "{}" at exit 0 (one instance silently skipped) — that needs a kgsm-side change, not catchable here. — 1.25.0 — Fix: process args with spaces were mangled. ProcessRunner built a single space-joined ProcessStartInfo.Arguments string, which .NET re-splits on whitespace — so ANY argument containing a space (a multi-word console command like "say hello world", a config value, an instance name) reached kgsm as several argv and was silently truncated/corrupted. Both the sync and async exec paths now use ProcessStartInfo.ArgumentList (each arg a distinct, verbatim argv); the joined string is kept for logging only. Pre-existing latent bug, surfaced by the 1.24.0 console-input channel (the first surface to send free-form multi-word text). No API change. — 1.24.0 — Console-input event vocabulary + SendInput provenance. (1) instance_input_sent event type: new InstanceInputSentData (InstanceName + Command) for the event kgsm raises when an arbitrary console command is sent to a running instance via `kgsm instances input` / IInstanceService.SendInput. Unlike instance_config_changed (key only — secret hygiene), this carries the FULL verbatim Command text — a deliberate choice so the audit trail records exactly what an operator ran (console commands are admin-level); a consumer that must redact does so at its own boundary. Registered in KgsmJsonContext + EventService's type map. VOCABULARY ONLY — the lib emits nothing. (2) IInstanceService.SendInput gains optional (actor, origin) params (default null), stamping $KGSM_EVENT_ACTOR/$KGSM_EVENT_ORIGIN onto the one kgsm invocation via the shared KgsmProvenance helper (same pattern as SetInstanceConfigValue) so the emitted instance_input_sent event is attributable; null/empty values are omitted so kgsm keeps its honest fallback (actor → OS user; origin → none) — never fabricated. Additive and non-breaking for callers (the new params default to null); implementers of IInstanceService update the SendInput signature in lockstep. AOT-safe (registered type + plain env vars, no reflection). — 1.23.0 — BlueprintMetadata.RawgSlug (consumer side). Adds a nullable RawgSlug (string?) to the nested Blueprint Metadata object, mirroring the new metadata.rawg_slug field kgsm emits under the "Metadata" block on `blueprints info --json`. It is the game's RAWG.io catalog slug — a lookup HINT the control panel (kgsm-api) uses to fetch cover art / description / tags, the same kind of external-catalog id as steam_app_id; KGSM never calls RAWG. The blueprint name is NOT assumed to equal the slug (gmod→garrys-mod, ark→ark-survival-evolved); an unverified game stays null (a wrong slug = misattribution, forbidden by the no-fabricate invariant). BlueprintMetadata is already registered in KgsmJsonContext (PropertyNameCaseInsensitive) so there is NO context change — the JSON key binds automatically. Additive and non-breaking: JSON from older kgsm without the field deserializes RawgSlug as null, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (no reflection). Phase 2 of the RAWG cover-art/metadata integration. — 1.22.0 — Three additive, AOT-safe consumer changes for the Control-Panel API. (1) instance_config_changed event type: new InstanceConfigChangedData (InstanceName + Key, both always-present non-null strings) for the event kgsm raises when a single instance .config.ini key changes; the new VALUE is intentionally never carried (secret hygiene — config can hold passwords/tokens), so a consumer only learns which Key changed. Registered in KgsmJsonContext + EventService's type map. VOCABULARY ONLY — the lib emits nothing. (2) Watchdog console client methods on IWatchdogClient/WatchdogClient: GetConsoleTailAsync(name, lines) reads a finite oldest-first tail (GET /console/{name}?tail=N; a 404 unknown/non-native/no-console instance degrades to an EMPTY list, never throws — mirrors GetStatusAsync's 404→null), and FollowConsoleAsync(name) streams live appended lines (GET /console/{name}/follow, chunked) as an IAsyncEnumerable. The follow stream is UNBOUNDED and never self-completes (not on stop/EOF/missing-log) — it ends ONLY on the caller's CancellationToken; it runs on a SECOND HttpClient at Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan so the shared finite RequestTimeout cannot silently sever a long follow (ResponseHeadersRead alone does NOT exempt the body read from HttpClient.Timeout). Raw text, no new serialized type → no KgsmJsonContext change. (3) Tolerant UTC start_time converter: ProcessInfo.StartTime now binds via JsonTolerantUtcDateTimeConverter — a value with an explicit UTC Z (or offset) → a DateTime at Kind==Utc (kgsm-api's ServerAggregator accepts only Kind==Utc); ANY other string (the old local-time asctime "Sun Jun 21 23:53:46 2026", the offset-less "2026-06-16 14:23:01", empty, garbage) → null WITHOUT throwing — an offset-less value is honest null, never a fabricated instant (no TZ is assumed). Fixes whole-roster blanking: a single bad start_time used to throw inside the one bulk-status Deserialize and collapse EVERY server; now only that instance loses its own start_time. Per-property [JsonConverter] on an already-registered type → no KgsmJsonContext change. All three additive and non-breaking, reflection-free / Native-AOT-safe; an internal test-only WatchdogClient(HttpClient) ctor + InternalsVisibleTo(kgsm-lib.Tests) make the console paths unit-testable with a stub handler (no AOT impact). — 1.21.0 — UPnP port-forwarding event vocabulary (consumer side). Adds the two event data types the kgsm-watchdog raises when it opens/closes an instance's UPnP port mappings on the local IGD (router) via upnpc: InstanceUpnpOpenedData and InstanceUpnpClosedData, each carrying Ports as the canonical range-preserving List&lt;PortMapping&gt; ([{start,end,protocol}], the same shape `instances info --json` emits). These are DISTINCT from the host-firewall InstancePortsOpened/ClosedData (1.12.0): a router NAT forward is a different fact from a ufw rule — a host can have one without the other — so conflating them in one audit action would erase a real distinction. Emitted by the watchdog (the resident supervisor owns UPnP because it is process-lifetime state), stamped Actor="system"/Origin="system" (an autonomous daemon action); only a confirmed upnpc-exit-0 mapping emits, never a fabricated outcome. Both are registered in KgsmJsonContext and EventService's type map, so the C# receive path decodes them (kgsm-api audits them as network.upnp.open/.close). VOCABULARY ONLY — the lib emits nothing here; emission is the watchdog's (string-based EmitWithProvenance, reusing the canonical PortMapping.ToUfwSpec() to render the positional ports arg). Additive and non-breaking: the new types deserialize only when emitted, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (registered types + the already-registered List&lt;PortMapping&gt;, no reflection). — 1.20.0 — Native player-presence patterns on Instance (Increment 2). Adds Instance.PlayerJoinedRegex / PlayerLeftRegex ([JsonPropertyName player_joined_regex / player_left_regex]), mirroring the existing StartupSuccessRegex: KGSM materializes the blueprint's player_joined_regex / player_left_regex into the instance config, so `instances info --json` now carries them and the resident supervisor (kgsm-watchdog) reads them off this Instance to tail a NATIVE instance's log and emit instance_player_joined/_left itself (the .NET-Regex analog of the container in-image Perl shim). Empty when the blueprint sets no pattern → that detection is disabled (honest unknown). Additive and non-breaking: the fields default to "" when absent from the JSON, existing call sites compile unchanged; Instance is already registered in KgsmJsonContext so no context change, AOT-safe (no reflection). Also RESTORES Instance.EnablePortForwarding ([JsonPropertyName enable_port_forwarding], bool, default false): the watchdog's UpnpService is a live consumer of this per-instance UPnP gate, so removing the typed accessor (a prior big-refactor) stranded the watchdog at 1.10.0; current KGSM does not emit it (UPnP stripped from bash, re-homed to the watchdog per the headless-network plan) so it defaults false = forwarding off, exactly the current behaviour — restoring the field lets the watchdog consume current kgsm-lib without a UPnP migration. — 1.19.0 — Player-presence event vocabulary (consumer side). Adds the two event data types kgsm raises when a player joins/leaves a running instance: InstancePlayerJoinedData and InstancePlayerLeftData, each carrying PlayerId (string?) and PlayerName (string?) — both nullable because a source may give only a stable game-scoped id (SteamID64/MC UUID) OR a display name; the missing half is JSON null, never fabricated (at-least-one-non-null is enforced by the emitting shim, not the lib). For our kgsm-containers images these are forwarded by the kgsm-watchdog (tails the in-container NDJSON event channel and re-emits) stamped Actor="system"/Origin="system" — an autonomous observation; native detection is a later increment. Both are registered in KgsmJsonContext and EventService's type map, so the C# receive path decodes them (player-presence Increment 1 downstream — kgsm-api audits them as player.join/player.left). VOCABULARY ONLY — the lib emits nothing here; emission is the watchdog's (string-based EmitWithProvenance, no lib change needed to emit). Additive and non-breaking: the new types deserialize only when emitted, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (registered types, no reflection). — 1.18.0 — Force uninstall (non-interactive). IInstanceService.Uninstall now passes --force to `kgsm uninstall`, so a programmatic uninstall never blocks on kgsm's interactive destructive-confirmation prompt — which, with no TTY, returned the new non-zero EC_CANCELLED and silently no-op'd, making a never-removed instance look "succeeded". The destructive confirmation belongs at the calling surface (the API's operator gate + the SPA confirm flow), not a TTY prompt to a non-interactive engine call. Requires the paired kgsm change adding the --force/-y flag to commands/uninstall.sh (and EC_CANCELLED on a declined interactive prompt). Behaviour-only — no signature or wire change, AOT-safe. — 1.17.0 — BREAKING: structured Blueprint.Ports + FromUfwSpec removed. Completes the canonical-port-format migration (1.10.0 did instances) to the blueprint surface: KGSM now emits `Ports` as the structured [{start,end,protocol}] array on `blueprints … --json` (the SAME shape `instances info --json` emits), so Blueprint.Ports changes from an opaque UFW string to List&lt;PortMapping&gt; — one ecosystem port type everywhere, no consumer re-parses a string. The 1.16.0 PortMappingExtensions.FromUfwSpec parser is REMOVED: its only purpose was to re-parse the blueprint string in C#, now obsolete at the source (hard cutover, no dead code, consistent with the 1.10.0 port-format and 1.13.0 de-ufw removals). List&lt;PortMapping&gt; was already registered in KgsmJsonContext (from 1.10.0) so there is no context change; start/end bind via the existing global string→int coercion (either wire shape works). Blueprint.ToString() renders Ports via .ToUfwSpec(). Migration: a consumer that read Blueprint.Ports as a string renders the legacy form with .ToUfwSpec(); the only workspace consumers (kgsm-api library catalog, kgsm-bot blueprint projection) are updated in lockstep. AOT-safe. — 1.16.0 — Parse the legacy UFW port-spec string (PortMappingExtensions.FromUfwSpec). Adds the inverse of ToUfwSpec: a static parser turning a UFW-style spec ("26900:26903/tcp|26900:26903/udp", "7777/udp", a proto-less "34197") into the canonical List&lt;PortMapping&gt; — single port (start==end), inclusive range (start:end), and a proto-less entry expanded to one tcp + one udp mapping (mirroring KGSM's UFW expansion and the existing PortMapping doc note). It lives at the kgsm-lib chokepoint precisely so no consumer re-parses an opaque port string: KGSM still emits ports as the legacy string on the `blueprints … --json` surface (unlike `instances info --json`, which is already structured), so the control-panel API's blueprint catalog now gets a structured value here instead of relaying a string the SPA would have to split. Honest/robust: a malformed entry (non-numeric port, unknown protocol, inverted end&lt;start range) is skipped defensively rather than guessed (the same posture as Expand), and a null/blank spec yields an empty list. Additive and non-breaking: pure new logic, no wire-type or signature change, AOT-safe (no reflection). — 1.15.0 — Provenance on every mutation (not just lifecycle). The optional (actor, origin) pair that 1.8.0 added to ILifecycleService.Start/Stop/Restart is now also on IInstanceService.Install/Uninstall/Update/CreateBackup/RestoreBackup/SetInstanceConfigValue, and on IInstanceService.Start/Stop/Restart (which forward to the lifecycle layer) — so a consumer (kgsm-bot, the assistant Service) can attribute EVERY server-mutating action it issues, not only start/stop/restart. Each stamps $KGSM_EVENT_ACTOR/$KGSM_EVENT_ORIGIN onto the one kgsm invocation via a new IKgsmCommandExecutor.Execute(environment, timeout, args) overload (env + custom timeout, for the long-running install/update/backup/restore/uninstall verbs; config-set uses the existing default-timeout env overload). The shared rule lives in one internal KgsmProvenance.Build helper (extracted from LifecycleService): a null/empty actor or origin is omitted so KGSM keeps its own honest fallback (actor → OS user; origin → none) — never fabricated. kgsm's event layer (commands/events.sh) already reads these for every event, so no kgsm bash change is needed. Additive and non-breaking: the new params default to null, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (plain env vars, no wire-type change). — 1.14.0 — Firewall enforcement state (consumes Firewall.Contracts 1.1.0). Surfaces the authority's new enforcement axis so a consumer never reads an inactive firewall as "closed": FirewallListResult gains Enforcement (new FirewallEnforcement enum: Enforcing | Inactive | Unknown) — when Inactive the backend filters NOTHING, so every port is reachable regardless of owned rules; FirewallService maps it from the wire (a pre-1.1.0 authority omits it → Unknown, and the consumer falls back to prior behaviour). FirewallOutcome gains AppliedInactive ("applied-inactive") — a rule written/staged while the backend is inactive (a success: persists and enforces on the operator's next `ufw enable`, and the port is open meanwhile), distinct from an enforced Applied. Additive and non-breaking: the new enum members are appended (existing ordinals unchanged), Enforcement defaults to Unknown, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (these are code-mapped result types, not wire-deserialized — no KgsmJsonContext change). — 1.13.0 — BREAKING (de-ufw rename): IFileService.CreateUfw / RemoveUfw are renamed CreateFirewall / RemoveFirewall, matching the kgsm CLI cutover from the `files ufw enable|disable` verb to `files firewall enable|disable` (kgsm-firewall Inc 4 — the engine no longer renders ufw rules itself; the kgsm-firewall authority owns the host firewall and chooses the backend). The command strings these issue change in lockstep ("files ufw enable" → "files firewall enable", same for disable). Behaviour is otherwise identical — same KgsmResult, same exec path; only the method names + emitted verb changed. The "ufw" in PortMapping.ToUfwSpec and the firewall-backend token ("ufw"/"firewalld"/"none") are NOT affected — those name the stored port-spec format and the detected backend, not the renamed integration verb. Why a rename not an alias: the ecosystem does hard cutovers (no dead aliases), consistent with the 1.10.0 port-format and 1.3.0 systemd removals. — 1.12.0 — Firewall audit event vocabulary (instance_ports_opened / instance_ports_closed). Adds the two event data types kgsm now raises when it opens/closes an instance's host-firewall ports via the kgsm-firewall authority: InstancePortsOpenedData and InstancePortsClosedData, each carrying Ports as the canonical range-preserving List&lt;PortMapping&gt; ([{start,end,protocol}], the same shape `instances info --json` emits — never an opaque UFW string). Both are registered in KgsmJsonContext and EventService's type map, so the C# receive path decodes them (and kgsm-api can emit them via EmitWithProvenance(actor, origin=api) when it consumes this version). VOCABULARY ONLY — FirewallService still performs the firewall op and emits NOTHING; emission stays the caller's responsibility (kgsm bash command layer now; kgsm-api M6 on the C# side), kept out of the transport client so a missing event service can never silently drop the audit trail. Additive and non-breaking: the new types deserialize only when emitted, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (registered types + the already-registered List&lt;PortMapping&gt;). — 1.11.0 — Typed kgsm-firewall control client (IFirewallService / FirewallService): EnsureOpen / Remove / ListOwned / Backend over the host-firewall authority's newline-delimited-JSON unix socket (the authority is NOT HTTP, unlike the watchdog — so this speaks its line protocol directly, not SocketsHttpHandler). Consumes the new shared wire-contract package TheKrystalShip.KGSM.Firewall.Contracts (1.0.0) and maps the canonical PortMapping ↔ the wire PortDto at the boundary, so the ecosystem keeps its single port type; the dependency is one-directional (kgsm-lib → Firewall.Contracts, never the reverse — kgsm-firewall must not reference kgsm-lib, which would be a cycle). Result types preserve the authority's PRECISE outcome rather than a bare bool — FirewallActionResult.Outcome (applied/removed/noop/unsupported/failed) and FirewallListResult.Status with honest Unknown (the backend cannot answer is NOT "nothing open") — the C# analog of the authority's exit-code contract that kgsm-api's future honest "is this port open" verdict depends on. An unreachable authority (no socket / timeout / no reply) throws FirewallException, distinct from a reachable-but-unsuccessful result. Registered via AddKgsmFirewallClient (parallel to AddKgsmWatchdogClient). Reflection-free source-generated JSON (via the Contracts package), Native-AOT/trim-safe. This client performs the firewall op ONLY and emits NO kgsm events — the instance_ports_opened/instance_ports_closed audit events are emitted separately by the caller (kgsm-firewall Inc 3), kept out of the transport client so a missing event service can never silently drop the audit trail. Additive and non-breaking. — 1.10.0 — BREAKING: canonical structured port format. Instance.Ports changes from an opaque UFW-style string ("27015:27020/udp|27016/tcp") to List&lt;PortMapping&gt; — the range-preserving structured form [{start,end,protocol}] KGSM now emits on `instances info --json`. The redundant, pre-expanded Instance.UpnpPorts (bash-array-literal string) is REMOVED (consumers expand the structured form locally), and the unread ConfigurationInfo.Ports (status surface) is REMOVED. New PortMapping record (start/end ints, protocol "tcp"|"udp"; a single port has start==end, a proto-less UFW entry expands to one tcp + one udp mapping) and PortMappingExtensions: Expand() → individual (port, protocol) pairs (what upnpc / a per-port conflict scan need) and ToUfwSpec() → the legacy "start:end/proto|..." string. Registered in KgsmJsonContext (PortMapping + List&lt;PortMapping&gt;); start/end arrive as JSON numbers and bind via the existing global string→int coercion (either wire shape works). This is the single port type kgsm-firewall's EnsureOpen(instance, ports[]) and the watchdog's UPnP path consume — no consumer re-parses a port string. Why breaking-not-additive: a hard cutover (per the headless-network plan) so no dead string field or duplicate parser is left behind. — 1.9.0 — Watchdog crash/give-up events + provenance-stamped emit. Adds two event data types the resident supervisor (kgsm-watchdog) raises for autonomous lifecycle transitions the engine could not previously surface: InstanceCrashedData (instance_crashed — a desired-running process died unexpectedly and is being auto-restarted; warn) and InstanceFailedData (instance_failed — the supervisor exhausted its restart retries and gave up; the escalation signal). Both carry ExitCode (string; the literal "unknown" when unreadable — never a fabricated code) and Restarts (string; the consecutive-failure count), are registered in KgsmJsonContext + EventService's type map, and are stamped Actor="system"/Origin="system" (autonomous engine action, not a human surface). To let the watchdog stamp that provenance, IEventManagementService gains EmitWithProvenance(eventType, actor, origin, params) — a distinct method (not an Emit overload, to avoid resolution ambiguity with the existing params-only Emit) that layers $KGSM_EVENT_ACTOR/$KGSM_EVENT_ORIGIN onto the one kgsm invocation via the 1.8.0 IKgsmCommandExecutor environment overload, mirroring LifecycleService's provenance plumbing. Additive and non-breaking: new types deserialize only when emitted, existing Emit call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (registered types + plain env vars). — 1.8.0 — Event origin + caller-supplied lifecycle provenance. Completes the audit-provenance pair started in 1.6.0: the event envelope (EventWrapper) and EventDataBase now also surface Origin (string?) — the surface that drove the event (ui|assistant|discord|system|api), the companion to Actor (who) answering "through which surface". EventService copies it from the envelope onto each dispatched event-data object, like Actor/Timestamp. KGSM takes it from $KGSM_EVENT_ORIGIN; unlike the actor there is NO fallback for a bare CLI call (no product surface), so an absent origin stays null — never fabricated. To let a caller actually stamp this, ILifecycleService.Start/Stop/Restart gain optional (actor, origin) parameters that are propagated to KGSM as $KGSM_EVENT_ACTOR/$KGSM_EVENT_ORIGIN for that one invocation (new IProcessRunner/IKgsmCommandExecutor environment overloads layer them onto the child process, never mutating the lib's own environment); omitted values fall back to KGSM's own honest defaults. Additive and non-breaking: the new params default to null, a pre-origin payload deserializes Origin as null, and existing call sites compile unchanged. AOT-safe (Origin is a property of the already-registered EventWrapper; provenance is plain env vars). — 1.7.0 — Unified ecosystem health probe. WatchdogClient now probes the watchdog's GET /health (was /ready) for both IsReadyAsync and GetReadyAsync; the daemon serves /health as the canonical readiness path (200 ready / 503 + reason) and keeps /ready as a deprecated alias for one transition release, so a consumer still on 1.6.0 keeps working unchanged. Response shape is unchanged ({ready,detail} → WatchdogReadyState) and no method signature changed — additive and non-breaking. Part of standardizing every ecosystem leaf on a single GET /health. — 1.6.0 — Event audit enrichment (who/when). The event envelope (EventWrapper) now surfaces the top-level metadata KGSM already emits but the lib previously dropped: Timestamp (DateTimeOffset?), Actor (string?), Hostname (string?), and KgsmVersion (string?, bound from the wire's KGSMVersion). EventDataBase gains Timestamp + Actor, and EventService copies them from the envelope onto each dispatched event-data object — so handlers see who triggered an event and when, with no handler-signature change. Actor is the audit principal: KGSM takes it from $KGSM_EVENT_ACTOR (set by the calling surface — bot/assistant/watchdog) or falls back to the invoking OS user; null/absent is preserved as null, never a fabricated identity or time. Additive and non-breaking: a pre-enrichment payload leaves the new fields null. This is the consumer half of the event-enrichment work (kgsm emits, lib surfaces); event *persistence* lives downstream of the stateless engine. — 1.5.0 — Native cgroup path, plus the first package to ship three already-committed post-1.4.0 changes. NEW: Instance.CgroupPath — the per-instance cgroup v2 directory KGSM derives for a native instance (kgsm.slice/&lt;name&gt;), the exact path kgsm-watchdog (re)creates on every native start; empty for container instances (Docker owns their cgroup) and when KGSM omits the field. Lets kgsm-monitor read native cgroup counters directly (CPU/mem/pids/io) instead of the /proc process-tree fallback, without re-deriving the cgroup layout or opening the watchdog socket — additive, JSON without cgroup_path deserializes CgroupPath as empty. ALSO FIRST PACKAGED HERE (committed on main after the 1.4.0 cut): (1) Reading&lt;T&gt; typed-unknown envelope — **BREAKING**: GetAllStatuses now returns Dictionary&lt;string, Reading&lt;InstanceRuntimeStatus&gt;&gt; (was Dictionary&lt;string, InstanceRuntimeStatus&gt;); (2) InstanceService.GetInstanceConfigValue/SetInstanceConfigValue — read/write a single key in an instance .config.ini; (3) typed host SystemInfo model + ISystemService.GetSystemInfo(). — 1.4.0 — Blueprint game metadata. Blueprint gains a nullable Metadata object (BlueprintMetadata: DisplayName, Description, MaxPlayers, MinRamMb, RecommendedRamMb, BaseDiskMb) mirroring the new nested "Metadata" block emitted by `kgsm blueprints info --json`. All values are nullable: an unknown value is null, never a fabricated 0. Additive and non-breaking — JSON from older KGSM without the block deserializes Metadata as null. (KGSM itself moved to a unified per-game .bp.yaml blueprint format, but the lib only ever consumes the JSON wire shape, which is unchanged except for the added Metadata object.) — 1.3.0 — BREAKING: systemd removed as a KGSM instance lifecycle manager. Deleted the LifecycleManager enum and Instance.LifecycleManager/EnableSystemd/SystemdServiceFile/SystemdSocketFile/SystemdUnit, ConfigurationInfo.LifecycleManager, and the LifecycleManager field on the instance-started/stopped/restarted event payloads. Instance.Runtime (native|container) is now the sole supervision discriminator. Legacy lifecycle_manager/systemd_* JSON fields older KGSM still emits are tolerated (unmapped, ignored). — 1.2.0: Typed kgsm-watchdog control client (IWatchdogClient / WatchdogClient): start/stop/status/list/ready over the daemon's unix socket (HTTP/1.1 via SocketsHttpHandler.ConnectCallback), reflection-free source-generated JSON (Native AOT/trim-safe), registered via AddKgsmWatchdogClient. — 1.1.0: Bulk fleet status read (GetAllStatuses, with --fast); restart and *-failed event types; reflection-free source-generated JSON for Native AOT/trim safety.</PackageReleaseNotes>
